BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20140223T173000Z
DTEND:20140223T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20260318T055328Z
UID:b4dlvi6ebqogppcpj0kmbllo0s@google.com
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20140205T200907Z
DESCRIPTION:A book club for all readers: men\, women\, students\, teenagers
 . Meets one Sunday morning per month in the Rabin Library at Beth Sholom af
 ter morning Minyan\, at 9:30 a.m. All are welcome!\n\nNext Book: The Painte
 d Kiss by Elizabeth Hickey\n\nElizabeth Hickey's The Painted Kiss (2006)\, 
 a work of historical fiction about Gustav Klimt and the upper-middle class 
 Jewish girl who began as his art student and remained a life-long friend.  
 In a sense\, she was even his  protege\, because she became a noted dress d
 esigner in Vienna.  Although fictional\, I believe this book in fact follow
 s both their life stories very closely.\n\nHere is an excerpt from The New 
 York Times' review of The Painted Kiss:\n\nThe Painted Kiss tells the story
  of Emilie Floge\, who is remembered today for one fact and one possibility
 : she was among Vienna's most exclusive couturieres between the two world w
 ars and she may have been the lover of the great Austrian artist Gustav Kli
 mt. Although she is a real historical figure\, so little is known about her
  personal life that Elizabeth Hickey is able to spend most of this first no
 vel simply inventing her. \n\nThe Painted Kiss is an investigation of art a
 nd artifice. As a designer\, Emilie is familiar with ''a million tricks of 
 drape and cut.'' Unfortunately for her\, she is also very good at hiding he
 r lifelong passion for Klimt\, aroused in 1886 when she is just a schoolgir
 l and he is commissioned to paint her portrait. \n\nViewing the finished ve
 rsion of his most famous painting\, ''The Kiss\,'' in which she and Klimt h
 ad been the original couple\, she discovers that he has substituted another
  figure for himself. ''He had painted me out\, too\,'' she adds. ''The girl
  in the painting had a sweet\, generic face. In vain I looked for my cheekb
 ones\, my strong jaw\, my thin\, wide mouth.'' \n\nMuch has been made of Kl
 imt's last words -- ''Send for Emilie'' -- but most art historians believe 
 the pair weren't lovers. Why\, then\, would this talented and beautiful wom
 an sacrifice romance and passion\, marriage and children\, for a man who ne
 ver openly declared his feelings for her -- and why would she remain faithf
 ul to his memory for so many years after his death? ''The Painted Kiss'' th
 rows this mystery into stark relief\, but it fails to penetrate Emilie's fa
 cade and come up with an answer.\n\nPlease join us if you are interested. W
 e welcome the suggestion of other titles.
LAST-MODIFIED:20140205T200907Z
LOCATION:Rabin Library 
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:CBS Book Group
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
