BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20160513T010000Z
DTEND:20160513T021500Z
DTSTAMP:20260318T055328Z
UID:u86ls773ds3oeqaaq714mrla4o@google.com
CREATED:20160403T215213Z
DESCRIPTION:The next meeting of the CBS Kids Book Club is Thursday\, May 12
 \, 6:30 - 7:15 p.m.\n\nParticipants will enjoy dinner followed by a discuss
 ion led by Howard Freeman\, a CBS congregant and Director of the Jewish Com
 munity Library\, as well as an opportunity for Q&A via Skype with Margaret 
 Peterson Haddix\, the author of Uprising\, the selected book for this sessi
 on.  \n\nCost: 10$ for the program and dinner OR 15$ for the program\, dinn
 er\, and a copy of the book (hopefully available for pickup at CBS by Thurs
 day\, April 7)\n\nPlease RSVP to Elin (elincohen@hotmail.com) and indicate 
 if you want a copy of the book or not. Please pay in cash to Elin. \n\nAbou
 t the book: \nThe fire at the Triangle Waist Company at the Lower East Side
  of New York City\, which claimed the lives of 146 young immigrant workers\
 , is one of the worst disasters since the beginning of the Industrial Revol
 ution\, and the disaster\, brought attention to work conditions and the lab
 or movement in America. Told from alternating points of view\, this histori
 cal novel draws upon the experiences of three very different young women wh
 o become friends: Bella\, who has just emigrated from Italy and doesn't spe
 ak a word of English\; Yetta\, a Russian Jewish immigrant and crusader for 
 labor rights\; and Jane\, the daughter of a wealthy businessman. Bella and 
 Yetta work together at the Triangle Shirtwaist Factory under terrible condi
 tions. When the situation worsens\, Yetta leads the factory's effort to str
 ike\, and she meets Jane on the picket line. Jane\, who feels trapped by th
 e limits of her own sheltered existence\, joins a group of high-society wom
 en who have taken an interest in the strike as a way of supporting women's 
 suffrage. All three of them are in the Triangle Shirtwast Factory on March 
 25\, 1911\, the day of the fateful fire.  The book is a forceful tale which
  puts a human face on a disasetous tragedy\, that would propel the rise of 
 the labor movement. While the book deals with some harsh labor conditions\,
  and the tragedy of the fire\, it also portraits friendships\, solidarity\,
  and the immigrant experience on the Lower East side. Our test readers (age
  11 and 12) HIGHLY recommend this book. 
LAST-MODIFIED:20160403T215213Z
LOCATION:Main Meeting Room
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:CBS Kids Book Club
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
