UID:gtausttf72k2qc76qsqc14aejs@google.com
CREATED:20160813T234620Z
DESCRIPTION:Although we think we know what Zionism means\, it is always hel
 pful to revisit its past and present\, as well as to consider its future. W
 hy Zion(ism) Matters\, a 30-minute talk by Ovid Jacob\, will provide attend
 ees with a taste of the 2016-17 CBS Lifelong Learning series of the same na
 me\, which will explore Zionism through lectures\, celebrations\, and art e
 xhibits.\n\nOvid Jacob escaped Communist Romania for Buffalo\, New York\, i
 n 1975. Today\, he works in high tech and devotes his leisure time to Jewis
 h ideas and culture. In addition to teaching a multi-week seminar on the re
 lationship between physics and Kabbalah here at CBS\, Ovid has presented gu
 est lectures in comparative religion (San Jose State) and in Daniel Matt’s 
 class on God and the Big Bang (Stanford).
LAST-MODIFIED:20160813T234811Z
LOCATION:Gronowski Family Chapel
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Ovid Jacob's Teaching
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20161211T170000Z
DTEND:20161211T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20260318T055328Z
UID:lmrt5bjpm0t7ba34u7lt6vs6jo@google.com
CREATED:20160926T165911Z
DESCRIPTION:Join Rabbi Aubrey Glazer and CBS congregant and writer\, Joan G
 elfand\, for a workshop on ethical wills. Bring pen and journal to be part 
 of this interactive workshop.\n\n“My father is not dead. My father is a boo
 k\, and books do not die.”\n\nWhat the late Elie Wiesel captured in a few w
 ords speaks to us all – the need to find a way to express our values and pa
 ss them on to the next generation through an ethical will (in Hebrew\, Zava
 'ah).\n\nAn ethical will is a love letter from one generation to the next\,
  hand crafted to pass ethical values from one generation to the next. In th
 e process of writing an ethical will\, each of us is asked to confront the 
 essential truths we have learned as individuals in a lifetime and consider 
 what really counts. Jews from all walks of life have continued to write eth
 ical wills during the nineteenth and twentieth centuries\, but the practice
  goes all the way back to Jacob gathering his children around his bedside\,
  trying to teach them how to live after he is gone. Each of us has wisdom o
 f experience that is unique and worthy of passing on to the next generation
 .\n\nFor more: http://bethsholomsf.org/e-m-weitz-breakfast-club-december-11
 /
LAST-MODIFIED:20161205T232431Z
LOCATION:Main Meeting Room
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Dr. Ernest M. Weitz Breakfast Club
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20180225T173000Z
DTEND:20180225T183000Z
DTSTAMP:20260318T055328Z
UID:6uja60vmpnuibua4lq90i54tin@google.com
CREATED:20180123T220026Z
DESCRIPTION:The next meeting of the CBS Book Group will be on Sunday\, Febr
 uary 25\, 2018 at 9:30 am to discuss Stefan Zweig's novel "The World Of Yes
 terday."\n\nStefan Zweig\, an Austrian Jew\, achieved acclaim at an early a
 ge as a poet and playwright and matured into one of the most read and trans
 lated novelists between the world wars.   He moved among European intellect
 uals\, artists and politicians.  Written in 1941-2\, these memoirs are an e
 yewitness to a European culture that flourished before WWI\, a culture heav
 ily influenced by Jewish participants and benefactors\, a culture that rebu
 ilt and redefined itself in a post-war\, post-Hapsburg Europe and then fell
  victim to Nazism.\n\nThe book is readily available in print\, e-book and a
 udio through the public library\, the SF Jewish Community Library and onlin
 e through Amazon.
LAST-MODIFIED:20180123T220026Z
LOCATION:Rabin Library
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:CBS Book Club
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
