BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20190428T163000Z
DTEND:20190428T180000Z
DTSTAMP:20260318T055328Z
UID:2gdmo8fg2gnb2hs49gjkommnrh@google.com
CREATED:20190424T211034Z
DESCRIPTION:This meeting of the CBS Book Group will be at 9:30am on April 2
 8\, 2019 (the Sunday after Pesach) to discuss the controversial "Submission
 " by Michel Houellebecq.  A satire in which fringe political ideology captu
 res power from the weak center.  Unlike books like Roth's "The Plot Against
  America"\, in which right wing politics use hate and fear to gain power\, 
 in Houellebecq's novel\, a moderate Islamic political party gains enough se
 ats in French Parliament to exert power over French policy.     The story i
 s narrated by a university professor who is witness to the political transi
 tion and must decide to adapt or rebel from the new reality. \n\nThe book i
 s readily available in print and e-book from the San Francisco Public Libra
 ry and on-line booksellers (Amazon.com).\n\n\nblurbs copied from Amazon.com
 \n\n“The political elements of Submission are so comically exaggerated that
  it's hard to take them very seriously . . . This is the novel's big joke. 
 It's designed to agitate the right by suggesting the right may have a point
  about the erosion of France's national culture\, and to tweak the left by 
 lending ironic credence to the right's fears . . . The only time Houellebec
 q seems not to be joking is when Francois speaks about literature . . . Wha
 tever it says or doesn't say about Europe and Islam\, Submission is a love 
 letter to the novel itself.” ―Christian Lorentzen\, New York Magazine\n\n“H
 ouellebecq's recent work―especially The Map and the Territory\, one of the 
 finest novels of the 21st century―is elegant\, sad\, all the more discomfit
 ing in that we never quite know how much subtlety to credit the author with
 . Houellebecq writes on shifting sands. But I think he might just be perman
 ent.” ―Michael Robbins\,The Chicago Tribune\n\n“In Submission\, Houellebecq
  is no less afraid to foment than in previous works\, but his audacity serv
 es a purpose that may not be immediately evident. His goal in this quasi-dy
 stopian novel is to cast a light on contemporary French society and the def
 iciencies he perceives and to suggest that the future he predicts isn't who
 lly beyond the realm of possibility . . . A challenging satire that\, at it
 s best\, is subtler than its author's reputation might lead you to expect.”
  ―Michael Margas\, The San Francisco Chronicle\n\n“Houellebec's deadpan com
 edic edge . . . defies the reader to find the line between parody and philo
 sophy . . . What Houellebecq has done in Submission is hold up a mirror to 
 his readers. The charge is that he inflames animosity by depicting a Muslim
 -influenced France as something of which Europeans should be frightened. Bu
 t he puts readers and critics in the position of having to specify what exa
 ctly is frightening about this France.” ―S. Mark Heim\, The Christian Centu
 ry\n\n\nReview from the Guardian\nhttps://www.theguardian.com/books/2015/se
 p/08/submission-michel-houellebecq-review-satire-islamic-france\n\nHouelleb
 ecq on Wikipedia:  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michel_Houellebecq
LAST-MODIFIED:20190424T211659Z
LOCATION:Board Room
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:CBS Book Group
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
