UID:2v2kbk5nul9n121hd46kcldjvg@google.com
CREATED:20191028T195949Z
DESCRIPTION:<br><b>Please contact <a href="mailto:sundaybookgroup@bethsholo
 msf.org" id="ow9684" __is_owner="true">sundaybookgroup@bethsholomsf.org</a>
  for any questions about this event or future Sunday Book Group meetings.</
 b><br>The next  meeting of the CBS book group will be on Sunday\, November 
 24\, 2019 at 9:30am (the Sunday before Thanksgiving) to discuss the novel "
 The Immortalists" by Chloe Benjamin.<br><br>It's 1969 in New York City's Lo
 wer East Side\, and word has spread of the arrival of a mystical woman\, a 
 traveling psychic who claims to be able to tell anyone the day they will di
 e. The Gold children--four adolescents on the cusp of self-awareness--sneak
  out to hear their fortunes.  Both a dazzling family love story and a sweep
 ing novel of remarkable ambition and depth\, The Immortalists probes the li
 ne between destiny and choice\, reality and illusion\, this world and the n
 ext. It is a deeply moving testament to the power of story\, the nature of 
 belief\, and the unrelenting pull of familial bonds.<br><br><br>The book is
  widely available in print\, e-book and audio through the public library an
 d on-line book retailers.<br><br><br>blurbs:<br><br>An imaginative and sati
 sfying family saga... The author has written a cleverly structured novel st
 eeped in Jewish lore and the history of four decades of American life. The 
 four Gold siblings are wonderful creations\, and in Benjamin’s expert hands
  their story becomes a moving meditation on fate\, faith\, and the family t
 ies that alternately hurt and heal.<br>— Publisher's Weekly\,<br><br>THE IM
 MORTALISTS is magical... Benjamin [slips] into each of the characters’ head
 s and lets us live there for a while\, writing in a delicate third-person v
 oice that knows everyone’s secrets. There are moments as taut as a thriller
 \, where time disappears as you turn pages\; and passages of quiet compassi
 on\, as the characters reflect on the bonds of siblinghood\, on the idea of
  home\, on how those we have lost can still manage — miraculously and myste
 riously — to stay with us\, in ways that we can’t always explain.<br>— The 
 Seattle Times
LAST-MODIFIED:20191206T174846Z
LOCATION:Board Room
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Sunday Book Group
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
