CREATED:20181003T175332Z
DESCRIPTION:Weâre taking our learning on the road to Ocean Beach!\nAll He
 vruta Hebrew\, pre-Bânai Mitzvah students\, and post Bânai Mitzvah stud
 ents should join us to celebrate Lag baâOmer! Madrichim\, Kadima\, USY\, 
 and all faculty are also encouraged to join us. Find us at the north end of
  Ocean Beach\, where Fulton Street meets the water\, across from the Beach 
 Chalet. Parents\, youâre invited too!! \n\nWe'll play games\, sing songs\
 , learn a little something\, and of course\, there will be food! Fire is a 
 central component of Lag baâOmer celebrations so we will have hot dogs an
 d sâmores (youâll do your own cooking). Please wear layers as we all kn
 ow SF weather fluctuates!\n\nWhat is Lag baâOmer? Lag BaâOmer is the 33
 rd day of the counting of the Omer (the days between Pesach and Shavuot). L
 ag is composed of the Hebrew letters lamed and gimel. In Hebrew\, each lett
 er has a numerical value\; lamed = 30 and gimel (the third letter of the al
 eph-bet) = 3. Therefore\, Lag = 30 + 3 = 33. The 33rd day of the Omer. We c
 ount the days between Pesach and Shavuot to remind us of the link between P
 assover\, which commemorates the Exodus and Shavuot\, which commemorates th
 e giving of the Torah. It reminds us that the redemption from slavery was n
 ot complete until we received the Torah. This is traditionally a quiet time
  on the Jewish calendar\, but Lag baâOmer is an exception\; bonfires\, ou
 tdoor parties and revelry rule the day\, which is also a popular Jewish wed
 ding date.
LAST-MODIFIED:20190124T235611Z
LOCATION:Ocean Beach\, San Francisco\, CA\, USA
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:LAST Hevruta Hebrew (grades 2-6 & EVERYONE IS INVITED)
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
