DTSTART:20190818T163000Z
DTEND:20190818T180000Z
DTSTAMP:20260318T055328Z
UID:48qtlblvme24lmcooq2ib9tbs4@google.com
CREATED:20190723T193658Z
DESCRIPTION:The next meeting of the CBS Book Group will discuss Nathan Engl
 ander's latest novel "Kaddish.com". <br><br>In this comic novel\, Larry\, a
 n atheist with an orthodox upbringing\, appalls his family when he announce
 s has no plans to say kaddish for his father.  To placate his mother and hi
 s sister\, he arranges a proxy through the website Kaddish.com.  Although t
 he plan to hire a proxy mourner was to help his father's soul find rest\, i
 t takes Larry places he hadn't imagined.<br><br>This book is available in p
 rint\, e-book and audio forms from the San Francisco Library and on-line re
 tailers.<br><br><br><br>blurbs (copied from <a href="http://amazon.com">ama
 zon.com</a>)<br><br>"Sublime...<a href="http://kaddish.com">kaddish.com</a>
  reads like a modern-day Hasidic tale in which religious characters are bed
 eviled by the challenges of upholding God's word in an all too human world.
 .. Kafka and Roth's influences are felt in Englander's work... In this tend
 er\, wry and entertaining novel\, Englander nimbly juggles these possibilit
 ies\, creating an endearing hero who stumbles through a world in which the 
 holy and profane are intertwined."<br>—The New York Times<br><br>"The humor
  of <a href="http://kaddish.com">kaddish.com</a> is infused with delight...
  What a rare blessing to find a smart and witty novel about the unexpected 
 ways religious commitment can fracture a life — and restore it."<br>—Washin
 gton Post"[An] excellent comic dissection of Jewish-American life...This no
 vel reads like Chaim Potok filtered through the sensibility of Mel Brooks. 
 Englander writes cogently about Jewish-American assimilation\, and\, in his
  practiced hands\, he makes<br><br><br>About the Author (from Amazon.com) <
 br>Nathan Englander is the author of the story collections For the Relief o
 f Unbearable Urges\, an international best seller\, and What We Talk About 
 When We Talk About Anne Frank\, and the novels The Ministry of Special Case
 s and Dinner at the Center of the Earth.  His books have been translated in
 to twenty-two languages\, and he is the recipient of a Guggenheim Fellowshi
 p\, a PEN/Malamud Award\, the Frank O'Connor Award\, the Sue Kaufman Prize 
 from the American Academy of Arts &amp\; Letters and was a Finalist for the
  Pulitzer Prize in 2013. His play The Twenty-Seventh Man premiered at the P
 ublic Theater in 2012. He is Distinguished Writer in Residence at New York 
 University and lives in Brooklyn\, New York\, with his wife and daughter.<b
 r><br>For more information\, please contact the group organizer at SundayBo
 okGroup@BethSholomSF.org
LAST-MODIFIED:20190816T180428Z
LOCATION:Board Room
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:CBS Book Group -- Kaddish.com
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20191125T010000Z
DTEND:20191125T033000Z
DTSTAMP:20260318T055328Z
UID:51frrkhkb7amhd9u72rv9k20b0@google.com
CREATED:20190913T174520Z
DESCRIPTION:<br><b>The&nbsp\;Heartbreak&nbsp\;Kid&nbsp\;</b>paints an unfla
 ttering picture of the Jewish American Male\, circa 1972. Our hero\, Lenny 
 Cantrow (Charles Grodin in top form) dumps his wife (an oddly sympathetic J
 eannie Berlin) on their honeymoon to pursue a younger\, blonder\, and infin
 itely WASPier woman (Cybill Shepherd\, representing the apotheosis of gorge
 ous shiksa goddess superiority). Is this a work of anti-semitic and misogyn
 ist self loathing by director Elaine May (with assist by writer Neil Simon)
  or a satire of the stereotype of the striving\, amoral Jew\, bent on assim
 ilation through sheer force of will? Either way this movie cuts deeper and 
 takes more risks than what we usually expect from a Hollywood comedy.&nbsp\
 ;<br>Jewish types and stereotypes in American film <br>This series of scree
 nings explores the work of prominent film directors that have pointed the c
 amera at the American Jewish experience of which\, they themselves were a p
 art. Rabbi Ain\, in conversation with CBS congregant and film buff Ben Chin
 n\, will lead a discussion following each movie. Film selections will inclu
 de works by Joel and Ethan Coen\, Mel Brooks\, Mike Nichols\, and other fil
 mmakers who have created their own Jewish texts out of celluloid. Dinner is
  included. Films may include: A Serious Man\; The Frisco Kid\; The Producer
 s\; Zelig\; Biloxi Blues\; The Heartbreak Kid\; and more.  <br><br><br>Pric
 e: Public $36/film or $180 for the series<br>CBS Members $18/film or $108 f
 or the series<br>Location: Koret Hall<br><br>Future Dates:<br>December 25\;
  Family-friendly movie &amp\; Chinese food<br>January 26<br>February 23<br>
 March 22<br>April 19 <br>May 31
LAST-MODIFIED:20191120T074135Z
LOCATION:Koret Hall
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Dinner and a Movie: The Jewish Frame "The Heartbreak Kid"
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20191117T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20191117T140000
DTSTAMP:20260318T055328Z
UID:4tltno394r6111ck49b6gfdf8d@google.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20191117T140000
CREATED:20190913T185431Z
DESCRIPTION:The Kitchen Hevruta\nwith Gabi Moskowitz & Evan Wolkenstein\n\n
 One of the great things about Torah is that every story operates both as a 
 narrative and as a kind of metaphorical filter to help us see and name the 
 world around us. Whenever you’re studying the Torah\, in other words\, you’
 re also studying human nature and unpacking an ancient and wise way of unde
 rstanding the world.\n\nOther disciplines operate in the same way: when you
  learn how to cook\, you’re not just learning how to combine ingredients to
  make a great dish. You’re learning ways to see the world. How to find bala
 nce. How to surprise\, delight\, nourish\, and satisfy. And you’re learning
  that it’s not just the food - but also who feeds us - that gets us fed.\n\
 nThis four part series will use a structure of 1) hands on prep\, 2) while 
 waiting for the food to cook/heat/rise/sit\, a lively and spirited text dis
 cussion\, and 3) finishing the food - tasting and reflecting on the lessons
  of the day - both culinary and intellectual/spiritual.\n\nCaramel and the 
 Sticky Path to Redemption\nCauliflower Crust Pizza and the Holy Trickster\n
 Crispy Korean Chicken Wings/Tofu and Mirroring at Sinai\nYay\, Leftovers & 
 Mining/Undermining\n\nPrice: Public $360 | CBS Members $72\nLocation: CBS K
 itchen & Main Meeting Room\nTime: 2 p.m.\nDates: Sundays. Nov 17 & 24 and D
 ec 8 & 15
LAST-MODIFIED:20191121T190658Z
LOCATION:Congregation Beth Sholom\, 301 14th Ave\, San Francisco\, CA 94118
 \, USA
SEQUENCE:2
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:The Kitchen Hevruta
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
