DESCRIPTION:<html-blob><u></u><u></u><p>Rabbi Lew (z"l) wrote: Tisha B'Av c
 ommemorates the fall of the first and second Temples in Jerusalem and\, by 
 extension the whole gamut of disasters that have befallen the Jewish people
 . Tisha B'Av is not about victimization. Both as individuals and as a peopl
 e\, we are called by Tisha B'Av to acknowledge the dark side of our existen
 ce and to take responsibility for it as well.</p>As such\, it makes an exce
 llent starting point for our High Holy Days preparations. Tisha B'Av is abo
 ut our estrangement from God\, from ourselves and from others. Rosh Hashana
 h and Yom Kippur\, which begin exactly seven weeks after Tisha B' Av\, are 
 about reconciliation\, atonement and <i>teshuvah</i>. On the deepest level\
 , Tisha B'Av and the High Holy Days are part of the same process.</html-blo
 b><br><html-blob><br></html-blob><br><html-blob><u></u>Join Congregation Be
 th Sholom on Zoom for Sunday morning minyan\, including a full reading of E
 icha (Book of Lamentations).&nbsp\;<br><br><u></u>Join Zoom Meeting: <a hre
 f="https://us02web.zoom.us/j/83431753002">https://us02web.zoom.us/j/8343175
 3002</a><br><br>Meeting ID: 834 3175 3002<br><br>Call in: (669) 900 6833 <b
 r>&nbsp\;&nbsp\;<br><u></u><u></u></html-blob>
LAST-MODIFIED:20220721T235143Z
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Sunday Morning Minyan and Tisha B'Av
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
