BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20211006T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20211006T210000
RRULE:FREQ=WEEKLY;WKST=SU;UNTIL=20211029T065959Z;BYDAY=WE
DTSTAMP:20260318T055329Z
UID:17v7fudunnol7fq71st2sakl0i@google.com
CREATED:20210924T200305Z
DESCRIPTION:<p><b>ScholarStream: Cultivating Our Relationships</b></p><p>Bi
 blical stories can inform our understanding of our own relationships. The l
 ife lessons of our ancestors influence our personal\, communal\, and world 
 values.</p><p><b>10/6: Dr. Alan Cooper\, “God\, Abraham and Isaac: Who is t
 he ‘Hero’ of the `Aqeda?”&nbsp\;</b><br></p><p>The story of the “binding of
  Isaac” (`aqeda) in Genesis 22 is a tale of problematic relationships\, tho
 se among the characters as well as the reader’s relationship with a troubli
 ng and alienating story.</p><p>How can God issue such a shocking and callou
 s command to Abraham\, a faithful follower?&nbsp\; Why does Abraham comply 
 without objection\, in contrast to previous interactions with God?&nbsp\; A
 nd why does Isaac remain passive and apparently acquiescent with his life a
 t stake?&nbsp\; Is there a hero in this story\, in the sense of a character
  who is to be celebrated for his actions\, and what are we supposed to lear
 n from him?&nbsp\; We will take up those questions in the light of both tra
 ditional and modern Jewish commentary.<br></p>
LAST-MODIFIED:20210924T200340Z
