BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20110409T193000Z
DTEND:20110409T203000Z
DTSTAMP:20260318T055329Z
UID:509t41or2cp9o8om03geatqj74@google.com
CREATED:20110328T183517Z
DESCRIPTION:The philosopher Jean-Paul Sartre has always been a controversia
 l figure. Humanist\, atheist and later an avowed neo-Marxist\, he stood as 
 a hero for secular socialism. Sartre\, however\, had a history of changing 
 conceptual gears and this was never more evident than at the end of his lif
 e. In a series of interviews with his secretary Benny Lévy\, entitled Hope 
 Now\, Sartre shocked the intellectual establishment by rejecting key elemen
 ts of Marxism and\, in their place\, adopting a number of principles from J
 udaism to espouse what he took to be his most highly developed form of huma
 nism. How did this extraordinary state of affairs come about\, and what exa
 ctly about Judaism was it that Sartre incorporated into his own work?\nAdri
 an Mirvish has discussed these issues in a recently published anthology ent
 itled “New Perspectives on Sartre”. Join us at Congregation Beth Sholom on 
 April 9th\, after Kiddush at 12:30\, to hear what Prof. Mirvish has to tell
  us about this fascinating topic.\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20221210T080130Z
LOCATION:Rabin Library
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:“New Perspectives on Sartre” with Professor Adrian Mirvish 
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
