BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20130721T163000Z
DTEND:20130721T173000Z
DTSTAMP:20260318T055329Z
UID:ee16bf20qbu69spiddau38sqg8@google.com
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20130621T181525Z
DESCRIPTION:The Next Meeting: is Sunday\, July 21 (Sunday @ 9:30AM)\nThe In
 visible Bridge by  Julie Orringer\n\nThis book is a good summer-read. Much 
 lighter than anything we have recently read. But\, oddly enough\, it is a H
 olocaust Book...... about the Jews in Hungary\n\nThe NY Times Book Review i
 n 2010 gave the author high marks on her first\, full length novel. "We all
  know what happened in the Holocaust.........The [Book] provides another li
 terary glimpse of the day-to-day horrors of that time..."  "Orringer....mak
 e[s] us care so deeply about the people of her all-too-real fictional chara
 cters. For the time it takes to read this fine novel\, and for a long time 
 afterward\, it becomes our world too."\n\nIt is about the life of a young\,
  Jewish-Hungarian architecture student who begins his studies in Paris in "
 1939"\; and the daily awareness and assessment of the changes in France\, E
 urope and the World that urbanized\, and some well-off\, Jews in France and
  Hungary experience.  The first half of the book takes place mostly in Fran
 ce and then we see the effect the impending and actual War has on the two m
 ain families and other characters.  It is all too real\, yet very different
  from any other Holocaust account that I am aware of. In  the final analysi
 s\, it can certainly be described as "a love story". \n\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20221210T081109Z
LOCATION:Rabin Library
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:CBS Book Club
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
