CREATED:20101015T174435Z
DESCRIPTION:CBS is proud to invite you for our weekly Talmud study\nThursda
 ys\, 7:15 p.m. in the Board Room.\n\nThe class will try to answer the quest
 ion:\n\nTalmud: What Did the Rabbis Mean?\n\nIn this class we will explore 
 rabbinical ideology\, theology and philosophy as they underlie Talmudic dis
 cussions. Each meeting will try to understand a specific piece of text and 
 will discuss the rabbinic ideology that it reflects. Come and learn the eth
 ical and philosophical perspectives of the Rabbis\, and see what the most d
 iscussed and studied text of Judaism is actually about.\n\nThis class is ge
 ared towards people who have some background in Talmud\, but due to the foc
 us on ideology it is accessible to beginners as well. Each week we will dis
 cuss an independent unit of text\, so feel free to jump in whenever you can
 .\n\nIn the coming weeks we will discuss:\n\nMarch 3\nWhen can we allow a d
 ispute: following the stories about Rabban Gamliel and Rabbi Yehoshua. (foc
 using on Berachot 27B-28A).\n\nMarch 10\nEducation policy:\, should we have
  limits on who shall attend our schools. (focusing on a paragraph from Bera
 chot 28A)\n\nOn March 17\nWhat the Rabbis do when they feel uncomfortable w
 ith a torah ruling: (focusing of the following paragraph from Berachot 28A)
 \n\n\nClass is led by Rabbi Avi Novis-Deutsch\, a Masorti scholar visiting 
 the Bay Area. Rabbi Novis-Deutsch studied Talmud eight years in Yeshiva\, a
 nd an additional six years in the Hebrew University and SIJS. Prior to his 
 arrival in the Bay Area he taught Talmud and Halacha at the Israeli Rabbini
 cal Seminar "Schechter Rabbinical School"\, Jerusalem.\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20221210T080706Z
LOCATION:Board Room
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Weekly Talmud Study
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20110521T003000Z
DTEND:20110521T013000Z
DTSTAMP:20260318T055329Z
UID:3db8pp9kumrlq7r3uvii8sijv0@google.com
CREATED:20110407T185149Z
DESCRIPTION:Our wonderful PJ Shabbat and PJ Havdallah programs invite famil
 ies with young children to join our vibrant Shabbat and Havdallah services.
  Families participate in singing\, music\, dancing\, and blessings with Rab
 bis Hyman and Philmus. The interactive services allow even our youngest par
 ticipants to experience the joy of Shabbat in a warm\, welcoming setting. F
 amilies will take with them some of the basic rituals they can use to enhan
 ce their own Shabbat\, Havdallah\, and holiday observances at home. All ser
